\newlabel{fig:dissociation}{{2}{4}{\textbf {Local flatness and global multiplicity diverge at grokking.} \emph {Left:} SGLD-LLC (purple, sensitive to global multiplicity) and Hessian-estimated LLC $\hat \lambda _H$ (teal, sensitive to local flatness) on a shared log axis, with test accuracy (green dots). Both are high during memorisation; at the grokking transition $\hat \lambda _H$ collapses while SGLD-LLC stays elevated—two orders of magnitude apart post-grokking. The value $\hat \lambda _H\!\approx \!18$ matches the intrinsic dimensionality of the Fourier circuit ($\sim \!12$ frequencies $\times \,2$ parameters). \emph {Right:} Ratio of the two estimators over time. The ratio jumps at the grokking transition and remains elevated—a geometry-based phase detector that requires no labelled test set}{figure.caption.2}{}}
\newlabel{fig:dissociation@cref}{{[figure][2][]2}{[1][3][]4}{}{}{}}
